      "heading": "Module Recap and Common Mistakes",
      "html": "<p><strong>Takeaways:</strong></p>\n<p><ul><li>The compressor squeezes low-pressure gas into hot, high-pressure gas.</li><li>Most compressors are belt-driven through an electromagnetic clutch that cycles on and off.</li><li>Rapid cycling often points to low charge; no engagement may be electrical or a safety cut-out.</li><li>Compressors pump gas only — liquid slugging can break them.</li><li>Recover refrigerant before opening the system; never vent it.</li></ul></p>\n<p><strong>Common mistake:</strong> Replacing a compressor without finding why it failed. If debris or a blocked expansion device killed the first one, it will kill the second one too.</p>\n<p><strong>Ready-to-Work link:</strong> If you can explain what the clutch does, what rapid cycling means, and why liquid must never reach the compressor, you have this module.</p>\n"
